{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"e1048dd956b4476c","type":"text","text":"[[Stoicism]] is the Ancient Greek philosophy of following virtues, keeping integrity, being indifferent to pain and pleasure. Ultimately the quest for a eudaimonic life.","x":-125,"y":-30,"width":525,"height":190},
		{"id":"66d77c6c6e41361f","type":"text","text":"**1. Through work/deed**\nOne can find love in their work\nExample: A doctor who is passionate about aiding their patients.","x":-2118,"y":-1627,"width":414,"height":127},
		{"id":"4d992b0872c6c3a8","type":"text","text":"**2. Love**\nOne can find love in another person.","x":-2054,"y":-1447,"width":286,"height":102},
		{"id":"b53006a79fdb6764","type":"text","text":"[[Logotherapy]] is a type of pyschological therapy to find meaning in one’s life. Achieved in 3 ways.","x":-1382,"y":-1357,"width":546,"height":100},
		{"id":"eac2e6f704598e77","type":"text","text":"**3. Suffering**\nOne can find meaning despite being in a state of suffering.\n\nSuffering itself is meaningless, but if we are able to shift our perspectives, we can find meaning.","x":-2182,"y":-1307,"width":541,"height":161},
		{"id":"1c7a29af4f4a380f","type":"text","text":"**Universe Logos**\nThe universe","x":-1382,"y":-1157,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6e93df6a81f6ce92","type":"text","text":"**What we CAN control**\n- Our reactions in situations\n- How much we prepare for an exam\n- The amount of kindness we show everyday to others","x":-1558,"y":-335,"width":426,"height":184},
		{"id":"0b98f239f73b5d6e","type":"text","text":"**What we cannot control**\n- Weather\n- Other peoples’ opinions\n- Death\n- Results of an exam","x":-1549,"y":-684,"width":440,"height":250},
		{"id":"297802173356eaf1","type":"text","text":"**Individual Logos**\nWe must try to align our thoughts with the [[Logos]]. Follow by:\n- Following the [[The Four Virtues of Stoicism]]\n- Being kind to others; just\n- [[Following nature]]","x":-1462,"y":-991,"width":367,"height":268},
		{"id":"f68a2b10932832f7","type":"text","text":"[[Dichotomy]] of control\n*We cannot control everything, but we can control how we react to situations*","x":-936,"y":-464,"width":386,"height":129},
		{"id":"1e906378f77760e6","type":"text","text":"[[Logos]] is the theory of a rational, ordered universe which acts as a living and intelligent entity.","x":-722,"y":-974,"width":320,"height":117},
		{"id":"d4ee21dcc587d2ba","type":"text","text":"[[Mortality]] is the reality that death is a natural process, and nothing to be frightened of.","x":-880,"y":180,"width":493,"height":134},
		{"id":"43b9b6539901af3b","type":"text","text":"***Memento Mori*** (remember you will die)\n\nPhrase to address the urgency of your life. You will inevitably die and there is nothing you can do about it. Therefore, we must use our lives as best we can and make the most of it.","x":-1753,"y":161,"width":390,"height":184},
		{"id":"5c77ed74097657d4","type":"text","text":"**Amor Fati (Love of Fate)**\n\n- Embrace good and bad things\n- Opportunity to practice the [[The Four Virtues of Stoicism]]\n- Removes any obstacle you have in the way. ","x":-1949,"y":391,"width":583,"height":185},
		{"id":"8da68ff039aaff61","type":"text","text":"Ultra common theme in [[Meditations]]. Marcus constantly refers to [[Mortality]] as he knows that he will die, even though he is one of the most powerful people in the world.","x":-1621,"y":691,"width":352,"height":155},
		{"id":"01a2223e5f6bd34d","type":"text","text":"We [[Should you be afraid of death?|should not be afraid of death]].\nIt is a natural process and you *cannot control it*. What is the point of being scared of it if it is out of your control?","x":-1621,"y":900,"width":395,"height":133},
		{"id":"6b2d23b8df322b55","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Pasted image 20260705163548.png","x":-1320,"y":-80,"width":400,"height":225},
		{"id":"04975ac344e87070","type":"text","text":"[[Good vs bad luck]]\n\n>[!QUOTE]\n>”It is my bad luck that this has happened to me. No, you should rather say: ‘It is my good luck that, although this has happened to me, I can bear it without pain … So why see more misfortune in the event than good fortune in your ability to bear it?”\n\nHere, we can control our reactions to bad or unlucky events by simply changing our perspective. Shifting from an attiude of “It is my bad luck that this has happened to me” you should instead focus on the good parts, “I can bear it without pain” is a way to demonstrate your resilience against an annoying/bad circumstance.","x":-2560,"y":-535,"width":683,"height":401},
		{"id":"9b0547edb6f8e07a","type":"text","text":"[[Judgement]] is the concept of assigning meaning to a thing/event.\nStoics believe that our suffering and emotional responses actually come from how we judge situations","x":740,"y":960,"width":400,"height":184},
		{"id":"2c18ff71aeff2102","type":"text","text":"By altering our [[Judgement]], we can have a peaceful state of mind.","x":1327,"y":895,"width":353,"height":105},
		{"id":"e8642f4092a5ed3b","type":"text","text":"[[Remove the judgement 'I am hurt']]\n\n>[!QUOTE]\n>Remove the judgement, and you have removed the thought ‘I am hurt’: remove the thought ‘I am hurt’, and the hurt itself is removed.”\n>4.7 - meditations\n\nPain is a matter of perception in the mind.\n\nApply to:\n- Cold showers\n- Temporary pain in muay thai (e.g. sting when kicking bag)","x":1460,"y":1080,"width":940,"height":440},
		{"id":"1b4fb1a53f8bdb36","type":"text","text":">[!QUOTE]\n>”We always live hic et nunc–here and now.”\n\nAnother brief phase of mortality.","x":-2460,"y":135,"width":417,"height":225},
		{"id":"26a023e5bcd73e3a","type":"text","text":"[[ataraxia]] is the concept of tranquillity of mind.","x":1960,"y":888,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"36080108d4471e14","type":"text","text":"**Zeno of Citium**\nWealthy merchant. \nLost all of his wealth in a storm on a boat.\nInstead of mourning and being depressed, he walked into a bookstore, read *Socrates* and developped the philosophy of Stoicism.","x":750,"y":-2040,"width":501,"height":188},
		{"id":"21d7470a82f1a7ac","type":"text","text":"[[Ryan Holiday]]\nActive author, book reader and Stoic.\nShares what he has learned in 15 years of Stoicism.","x":846,"y":-1819,"width":434,"height":119},
		{"id":"4f500da6cd913706","type":"text","text":"Key People","x":390,"y":-1787,"width":170,"height":60},
		{"id":"a8a9c3930b198462","type":"text","text":"[[Epictetus]]\nAncient greek philosopher, born as a slave.","x":872,"y":-1662,"width":408,"height":82},
		{"id":"13c61a1a057f7a44","type":"text","text":"[[Marcus Aurelius]]\nAncient Roman Emperor who practiced Stoicism frequently.\n\nCombatted two major wars, famine, plague and lost most of his children.\n\nNevertheless, he expressed many of his thoughts in a collection of journals called [[Meditations]]","x":810,"y":-1482,"width":510,"height":248},
		{"id":"2b3f819b8fd9f7e3","type":"text","text":"[[The Four Virtues of Stoicism]] are what we must follow everyday, and apply in every situation we encounter, nomatter how simple.","x":627,"y":-616,"width":380,"height":119},
		{"id":"fddb92e1e0965296","type":"text","text":"**Justice**\n- Be fair to others\n- Respect others even if they have done wrong\n\nExample: [[Nelson Mandela]] sat in a cell for 27 years. Yet, he did not punish his enemies when elected/. Instead he chose to ignore what he could not control–the past–and focus on the present.","x":1754,"y":-823,"width":859,"height":240},
		{"id":"09cc77cb4af8f2ed","type":"text","text":"**Temperance**\n- Keeping everything balanced\n- Balance other virtues–if you are only showing bravery but disrespecting others, you are out of balance","x":1777,"y":-523,"width":757,"height":161},
		{"id":"914f8d3cfd475e4a","type":"text","text":"**Wisdom**\n- Education\n- Constant pursuit of knowledge during life\n- Always be open to new ideas, feedback and pushbacks to your knowldge.\n- Example: Talking to another person about something you have just learned, as a way to see feedback straight away.","x":1777,"y":-263,"width":866,"height":220},
		{"id":"50f93847856aef74","type":"text","text":"**Courage**\n- Standing up for what we believe in\n- Put yourself out there, engage in new opportunities\n- Don’t be afraid of failure. it is all part of the process\n\nExample: Signing up for the DELF B2 Exam when you are definitely not even close to a french speaker, this shows courage. You were ready to accept a failure in equanimity.","x":1739,"y":-1143,"width":832,"height":225},
		{"id":"8e1c02b522952122","type":"text","text":"[[He who has a why to live for can bear almost any how]]\n\nNietzsche.\nIt takes courage to get through tough situations, but if [[Man’s search for meaning|one can find meaning within himself]] he can get through it.","x":2760,"y":-1205,"width":548,"height":166},
		{"id":"29057a3c404f5c55","type":"text","text":"[[Integrity]] is imperative in Stoicism. It is one of the most important aspects of a human. Losing your integrity is considered to losing your soul.","x":3064,"y":-124,"width":404,"height":162},
		{"id":"f6705be102c2b997","type":"text","text":">[!QUOTE]\n>”But for a Stoic, there is no trade off between A and B: nothing can be traded if the price is the compromising of your character.”\n>[[How To Be A Stoic]] - pp.76","x":3626,"y":-114,"width":444,"height":200},
		{"id":"bd816c75024daae2","type":"text","text":"![What Are the Four Virtues of Stoicism? | Ryan Holiday | Daily Stoic](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yaG7f3uivPU) \n\n[[What Are the Four Virtues of Stoicism - Ryan Holiday  Daily Stoic]]","x":1660,"y":0,"width":675,"height":379},
		{"id":"c1bff3b288a5cccd","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Pasted image 20260705162745.png","x":617,"y":-1101,"width":400,"height":398},
		{"id":"5a11836fc598226f","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Meditations-1781778967282.webp","x":1560,"y":-1819,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"42940d5d06eb694e","type":"text","text":"[[You do not have thousands of years to live]] - **Urgency**\n\nQuickly complete all tasks\nTreat every day as it were the last as a way to make urgency apart of your life","x":-2340,"y":621,"width":505,"height":225},
		{"id":"213de4189be0e242","type":"text","text":"[[Call strangers 'brother', 'sister' or even 'uncle']] is a bit of a physical way of showing respect to others. In practice, it is better to just treat strangers as a ‘brother’ or ‘sister’ rather than actually calling them that.","x":4070,"y":-1142,"width":532,"height":152},
		{"id":"76a53a69b69f923d","type":"text","text":"Being [[A good man]] is your goal","x":4167,"y":-877,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ac6c7200e19e9729","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Pasted image 20260705164729.png","x":4450,"y":-936,"width":400,"height":353},
		{"id":"7b636e8895a8b83c","type":"text","text":"[[oikeiôsis]] means ‘familiarzation’ or ‘appropation of’\n\nMeaning: We should treat other peoples’ concerns or problems as our own if they are in need of help. \n\nPerform this for nearly everyone, it is a great way to show kindnes..","x":4450,"y":-496,"width":620,"height":241},
		{"id":"2925541687f17698","type":"text","text":"[[Kindness]]\n- The aim of everyday is to be kind and treat people well\n- Today, give someone a compliment, or do something nice (e.g. laundry, dishwasher)\n- Always smile","x":3350,"y":-776,"width":720,"height":164},
		{"id":"f012cd600e642c2a","type":"text","text":"[[Respect]]\n- We must treat all with respect\n- Doesn’t matter if you dislike them, hate them or they have done wrong","x":3370,"y":-1122,"width":541,"height":186},
		{"id":"e2297cefab189118","type":"text","text":"Whenever something bad happens, [[don't make a big deal of things]].\n\n> [!QUOTE] \n> “Just see them as they are, in plain truth.”\n\nExample: You missed your train–that is fine, wait 10 minutes, patience is a virtue.","x":2959,"y":-523,"width":615,"height":250},
		{"id":"644b4c16c74a3c5b","type":"text","text":"[[Evil]]\nLargely caused by **ignorance** and not real evil.","x":-920,"y":1180,"width":475,"height":161},
		{"id":"aed3595c8639bf0a","type":"text","text":"[[amathia]]\n- Opposite of wisdom\n- Dis-knowledge of how to treat others → awful actions by regular humans\n- People with [[amathia]] cannot be persuaded by reasoned argument","x":-1570,"y":1199,"width":450,"height":201},
		{"id":"71865c9819dac1b6","type":"text","text":"”How we think about death is more important than dying itself”\n[[How To Be A Stoic]] - pp.158","x":-2640,"y":423,"width":422,"height":122},
		{"id":"006eb10da8c9c304","type":"text","text":"[[Cato the Younger]]\nDemonstrated his [[Integrity]] to the extreme","x":737,"y":-2263,"width":250,"height":103},
		{"id":"1b37012012c9db6a","type":"text","text":"**Seneca**","x":612,"y":-2520,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"2709dadfd5e639e5","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “[Seneca] explicitly advised taking a deep breath and going for a walk around the block  upon first feeling the uncontrollable rise of rage.”\n\nFantastic principle to control your anger and feel more relaxed.","x":1044,"y":-2617,"width":473,"height":255},
		{"id":"08947905b212b052","type":"text","text":"**Anger**\nIn any cases of anger, just walk around the block and take some deep breaths (Socrates)\n\nBeing angry will never make the situation better.","x":-1586,"y":1508,"width":250,"height":232},
		{"id":"8b630ae9499f6f66","type":"text","text":"[[Remind yourself that you are talking to a mortal]]\nEveryone you meet, talk to, befriend or love is a mortal. They will eventually die as will you, therefore, try not to be distressed when they are taken from you","x":-2534,"y":-80,"width":491,"height":165},
		{"id":"3dfdd24d6beb8f05","type":"text","text":"[[What to do if somebody has ‘attitude’ or snaps at you]]","x":-1937,"y":1569,"width":316,"height":91},
		{"id":"9f30a0d30e9fd733","x":1710,"y":-1338,"width":250,"height":104,"type":"text","text":"[[How to Journal (Like Marcus Aurelius)]]"},
		{"id":"912873abdd7a8113","x":2080,"y":-1727,"width":751,"height":382,"type":"text","text":"![How to Journal (Like Marcus Aurelius)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YOgBiweyEE4)"}
	],
